MIGRATIONS SQUASH FREELY UNTIL THE FIRST REAL FAMILY'S DATA IS IN A DEPLOYED DB;
ADDITIVE ONLY AFTER.

Before that line is crossed, the right response to a messy migration history is to delete
every file in versions/, drop and recreate the database, and regenerate one clean initial
migration. Nobody's data exists to lose, and a tidy schema is worth more than a tidy audit
trail. After it is crossed, every migration is additive — add a nullable column, backfill,
then tighten — because a downgrade on live family health data is not a thing that gets to
happen.


Running them
------------

Everything below runs from `backend/`.

    docker compose -f ../docker-compose.yml up -d
    export PENNY_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://penny:penny@localhost:5432/penny_test

    uv run alembic upgrade head
    uv run alembic downgrade base                          # round-trip check
    uv run alembic revision --autogenerate -m "what changed"

env.py takes its URL from `app.config`, not from alembic.ini: `-x url=...` beats `DATABASE_URL`
beats `PENNY_TEST_DATABASE_URL`. In production Railway runs `alembic upgrade head` as the
`penny` service's preDeployCommand, where only `DATABASE_URL` is set.


Autogenerate is a draft, not an answer
--------------------------------------

Read every generated file before committing it. Three things it gets wrong here:

  * Partial and expression indexes (`WHERE wa_jid IS NOT NULL`, `lower(display_name)`) are the
    idempotency guarantees of this schema, not tuning. If a diff proposes dropping one, the
    models are wrong, not the database.
  * Data migrations are never generated. A column that must be backfilled needs the backfill
    written by hand between the add and the NOT NULL.
  * A table whose model is not imported by `app/models.py` is invisible to the metadata and
    autogenerate will emit `op.drop_table` for it.

After generating, re-run `--autogenerate` once more: a second, empty migration is the proof
that the models and the database now agree. Delete that probe file.


Two branches, two migrations
----------------------------

Only Track B generates migrations before M4. Any other branch that needs one rebases onto the
latest head and regenerates. Never hand-edit `down_revision` to splice a file into the chain —
that is how you get two heads and an `alembic upgrade head` that silently applies neither.
